1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:02,359
The four-week pilot is complete.

2
00:00:02,539 --> 00:00:07,715
The manager brings forty eligible pull
requests from the baseline and forty from

3
00:00:07,715 --> 00:00:08,362
the pilot.

4
00:00:08,542 --> 00:00:13,382
First confirm that both sets use the same
eligibility and labels, and make any

5
00:00:13,382 --> 00:00:14,685
missing data visible.

6
00:00:14,865 --> 00:00:17,515
Pull requests are the unit for the
scorecard.

7
00:00:17,695 --> 00:00:21,684
Repeated reviewer observations belong to
one pull request.

8
00:00:21,864 --> 00:00:26,227
The results describe this fictional team's
service during these two windows.

9
00:00:27,673 --> 00:00:29,544
Wait time is a queue measure.

10
00:00:29,724 --> 00:00:34,032
It fell from a median of eighteen to
thirteen business hours, meeting the

11
00:00:34,032 --> 00:00:35,271
fourteen-hour target.

12
00:00:35,451 --> 00:00:39,241
Active review is the work someone spends
inspecting a change.

13
00:00:39,421 --> 00:00:43,834
It went from twenty-four to twenty-six
minutes and stayed within the twenty-seven

14
00:00:43,834 --> 00:00:44,543
minute limit.

15
00:00:44,723 --> 00:00:46,721
Tell this as two observations.

16
00:00:46,901 --> 00:00:52,117
The shorter queue is the measured benefit,
and active review still costs people time.

17
00:00:53,590 --> 00:00:57,439
Six of forty baseline requests needed
material rework.

18
00:00:57,619 --> 00:01:02,441
Seven of forty pilot requests did, equal
to seventeen and a half percent.

19
00:01:02,621 --> 00:01:05,014
That is above the fifteen percent ceiling.

20
00:01:05,194 --> 00:01:10,134
In this sample, all eighty requests merged
and had the same follow-up period.

21
00:01:10,314 --> 00:01:12,825
Each group had one post-merge defect.

22
00:01:13,005 --> 00:01:14,523
Those counts are small.

23
00:01:14,703 --> 00:01:20,063
The manager reads the seven pilot rework
cases and looks for patterns in the actual

24
00:01:20,063 --> 00:01:20,451
diffs.

25
00:01:21,923 --> 00:01:26,987
The pilot ledger attributes a hundred
dollars to licenses, twenty to remote

26
00:01:26,987 --> 00:01:32,320
workspace use for tests and CLI review,
and a hundred and twenty to getting the

27
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:33,198
team started.

28
00:01:33,378 --> 00:01:35,855
The total is two hundred and forty
dollars.

29
00:01:36,035 --> 00:01:40,419
Across forty eligible requests, that is
six dollars per request.

30
00:01:40,599 --> 00:01:43,845
Active review minutes are tracked
separately in this scorecard.

31
00:01:44,025 --> 00:01:48,399
A future cost model can include labor with
a clear allocation method.

32
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:52,709
Read the pull requests and their review
reasons.

33
00:01:52,889 --> 00:01:58,119
In four of the seven pilot rework cases,
the proposed change touched files outside

34
00:01:58,119 --> 00:01:58,821
the ticket.

35
00:01:59,001 --> 00:02:02,947
That gives the manager and tech lead a
concrete issue to discuss.

36
00:02:03,127 --> 00:02:06,945
Product telemetry may show that the team
used suggestions.

37
00:02:07,125 --> 00:02:11,499
The pull request evidence explains where
the review and rework happened.

38
00:02:11,679 --> 00:02:16,236
The team avoids drawing an individual
performance conclusion from these counts.

39
00:02:17,715 --> 00:02:22,152
The scorecard records shorter median wait
time and costs within the cap.

40
00:02:22,332 --> 00:02:27,420
It also records a quality guardrail miss,
a small sample, and a historical

41
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:28,176
comparison.

42
00:02:28,356 --> 00:02:31,004
That is enough to have a focused pilot
decision.

43
00:02:31,184 --> 00:02:36,047
Share the team-level figures, definitions,
and an example pull request.

44
00:02:36,227 --> 00:02:40,707
If a stakeholder asks whether the
assistant caused the shorter wait, explain

45
00:02:40,707 --> 00:02:44,893
which parts of the process and staffing
could also affect the estimate.
